\chapter{Introduction}
\label{ch:intro}

\section{Context}
Modern sports clubs --- particularly multi-discipline organisations that
operate football, basketball, handball and volleyball teams under one
governing body --- generate enormous volumes of operational data: training
sessions, match events, medical incidents, contract renewals, scouting
reports, sponsorship deals and fan engagement. In most clubs this data lives
in disconnected spreadsheets, third-party platforms and printed forms,
fragmenting the workflow of coaches, doctors, scouts and managers.

\section{Motivation}
The motivation behind MSCMS is to consolidate every operational concern of
a multi-sport club into one cohesive platform, where each professional role
sees only the information relevant to their job and every data point is
traceable, secured, and analytically rich. The design takes deliberate
inspiration from FC Barcelona's real organisational structure: a single
governing body (the club) managing four professional teams across football,
basketball, handball and volleyball, with shared medical, scouting and
sponsorship arms.

\section{Scope}
The system covers eight primary domains:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{User \& Role Management} --- 19 roles across four
        permission tiers, authenticated via Keycloak.
  \item \textbf{Player \& Team Operations} --- rosters, contracts,
        transfers (incoming, outgoing, call-ups), outer scouting prospects.
  \item \textbf{Training} --- sessions, plans, drills, attendance, and
        post-session player assessments.
  \item \textbf{Match Operations} --- fixtures, formations, line-ups,
        in-game events, post-match performance reviews, and player stats.
  \item \textbf{Medical \& Fitness} --- injuries, diagnoses, treatments,
        rehabilitations, recovery programs, fitness tests, training loads.
  \item \textbf{Reports \& Analytics} --- match analyses, team-level and
        player-level analytics, training analytics, scout reports,
        sponsor contract offers.
  \item \textbf{Notifications \& Alerts} --- targeted, role-aware,
        priority-tagged events delivered in-app or by email.
  \item \textbf{Machine Learning} --- match outcome prediction and player
        rating estimation served by dedicated FastAPI services.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Problem Definition}
\label{sec:problem-definition}
A modern multi-sport club struggles with a constellation of operational
problems that no single off-the-shelf tool addresses end-to-end:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Fragmented data across departments:} Medical staff,
        coaching staff, scouting, finance and sponsorship each maintain
        isolated spreadsheets, PDFs and SaaS tools. A player's injury
        record is invisible to the coach planning training, and a
        scout's report is invisible to the sport manager negotiating
        a transfer.
  \item \textbf{Multi-discipline complexity:} A single club may field
        football, basketball, handball and volleyball squads under one
        governing body, each with distinct positions, formations,
        seasons and KPIs that generic team-management tools cannot
        model simultaneously.
  \item \textbf{Inconsistent role-based access:} Tools that lack
        fine-grained RBAC either over-share data (a fan portal that
        exposes salary information) or over-restrict it (a doctor
        unable to read training-load data needed to plan rehabilitation).
  \item \textbf{Limited analytical insight:} Without a unified data
        layer, computing KPIs such as \emph{win-rate per training-load
        bracket}, \emph{injury rate per playing position}, or
        \emph{transfer ROI per scout} is impractical.
  \item \textbf{Absent ML predictions:} Few platforms expose
        match-outcome prediction or quantitative player rating models
        in a way that non-technical staff can consume directly.
  \item \textbf{Weak sponsor and transfer workflows:} Sponsor offers,
        contract negotiations and transfer windows are typically
        managed over email and PDF, with no audit trail or
        notification fan-out.
  \item \textbf{Poor fan engagement:} Fans cannot follow upcoming
        fixtures, results, squads or news through the same system the
        club uses internally, fragmenting communication and brand
        identity.
  \item \textbf{Manual notifications:} Critical alerts (injury, line-up
        change, transfer approval, sponsor decision) reach the relevant
        actor late or not at all because there is no role-aware
        notification fabric.
  \item \textbf{Brittle deployments:} Monolithic legacy systems force
        every domain (medical, training, sponsorship) onto the same
        release cadence, blocking unrelated feature work.
  \item \textbf{Inconsistent identity:} Without a central identity
        provider, every department maintains its own user accounts,
        making onboarding, off-boarding and audit slow and error-prone.
\end{itemize}

\section{Goals}
\label{sec:goals}
MSCMS pursues the following measurable, role-aware goals:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Unify operations} into a single platform spanning the
        eight domains in Section~\ref{sec:problem-definition}.
  \item \textbf{Enforce 19-role RBAC} consistently at three layers ---
        Keycloak (identity), API gateway (token verification), and
        Next.js middleware (route guards) --- so the same permission
        rules apply whether a user navigates via UI or calls APIs
        directly.
  \item \textbf{Decompose the back-end} into seven independently
        deployable Spring Boot microservices, discoverable through
        Eureka and reachable through a single API gateway.
  \item \textbf{Model real multi-sport structure} by seeding football,
        basketball, handball and volleyball squads, each with their own
        positions, contracts, fixtures and rosters, taking FC Barcelona
        as the working example.
  \item \textbf{Centralise analytics} into role-aware dashboards that
        aggregate team-, player-, match-, training- and medical-level
        data into a single overview surface.
  \item \textbf{Integrate machine learning} by serving match-outcome
        prediction and player-rating models from dedicated FastAPI
        services consumed transparently through the Next.js front-end.
  \item \textbf{Streamline sponsor and transfer workflows} by
        digitising sponsor offers, transfer windows, call-ups and
        contract approvals --- each producing notifications, audit
        events and analytics entries.
  \item \textbf{Deliver a polished fan experience} with read-only
        access to fixtures, results, squads, news and team data,
        themed to the modelled club's branding (Barcelona blue and red,
        Barlow Condensed typography).
  \item \textbf{Provide reliable notifications} through a dedicated
        notification-mail-service that fans out role-aware alerts in
        real time and as digestible in-app feeds.
  \item \textbf{Guarantee independent deployability} so each domain
        ships on its own release cadence behind backward-compatible
        API contracts.
\end{itemize}

\section{Solutions}
\label{sec:solutions}

\subsection{Traditional Solutions}
Historically, multi-sport clubs have relied on a combination of manual
and semi-digital tools to coordinate their operations. Coaching staff
typically maintain training plans and line-ups in spreadsheets or
printed binders, medical departments record injuries and treatments in
paper charts or local Excel sheets, and scouts compile player reports
as Word or PDF documents that circulate by email. Sponsorship and
transfer negotiations are conducted over email threads with attached
contracts, while fan communication is handled separately through
generic websites and social-media channels. This patchwork places a
heavy coordination burden on administrators: data is duplicated,
reconciliations are manual, and any change to a player's status (an
injury, a transfer, a contract renewal) requires multiple parallel
updates across disconnected silos. The approach scales poorly with the
number of disciplines (football, basketball, handball, volleyball) and
makes cross-domain analytics --- such as correlating training load with
injury frequency or transfer ROI with scout reports --- effectively
impossible. Furthermore, traditional approaches lack consistent
role-based access control: sensitive medical or financial data is
either over-shared via shared drives or over-restricted to the point
that legitimate staff cannot do their job.

\subsection{Technological Solution}
To overcome these limitations, we developed \textbf{MSCMS}, a
cloud-native multi-sport club management platform built on a modern
microservice architecture. The system unifies every operational concern
of a multi-discipline club --- football, basketball, handball,
volleyball --- into a single coherent interface, while preserving
independent deployability of each functional domain. The back-end
consists of seven Spring Boot microservices (user, player, training-match,
medical-fitness, notification-mail, reports-analytics, plus the
gateway/discovery/config infrastructure) and two FastAPI machine
learning services (match outcome prediction and player rating
estimation). All services are registered with a Eureka discovery
server, configured through Spring Cloud Config, and reached through a
single Spring Cloud Gateway that performs JWT verification, request
routing and CORS handling.

Authentication is delegated to Keycloak (realm: \texttt{mscms}) which
manages 19 distinct roles spanning club leadership (Admin, Sport
Manager, Team Manager), coaching staff (Head Coach, Assistant Coach,
Goalkeeper Coach), medical and fitness staff (Doctor, Physiotherapist,
Fitness Coach), analytics roles (Performance Analyst, Scout),
commercial roles (Sponsor), and end-users (Player, Fan, National Team).
JWTs issued by Keycloak are verified at the gateway and decoded by the
Next.js middleware to enforce the same permission map on both layers.
Each microservice owns its own PostgreSQL database, and asynchronous
events flow through Kafka and RabbitMQ, with Redis providing a shared
cache. Two ML services serve pre-trained joblib models behind a
Next.js rewrite proxy so the front-end can call them through the same
origin. The result is a scalable, observable, role-aware platform that
addresses the fragmentation, complexity and analytical blindness of
traditional approaches in one cohesive system.

