\chapter{Previous Work}
\label{ch:prevwork}

\section{Introduction}
MSCMS was developed to address the shortcomings of existing sports
management platforms and to provide a comprehensive solution for a
multi-discipline professional club. By analysing systems such as
\emph{Hudl}, \emph{Catapult Sports / Sports One}, \emph{SAP Sports One},
\emph{TeamSnap} and \emph{Kitman Labs}, we identified significant
limitations in role coverage, multi-sport modelling, role-based access
control, embedded analytics and integrated machine learning. This
chapter briefly reviews these platforms, highlights their drawbacks,
and compares them with MSCMS --- demonstrating how our system offers
superior coverage of the operational reality of a real multi-sport
club, including its medical, scouting, sponsorship and fan-facing
arms.

\section{Hudl}
\label{sec:prevwork-hudl}
Hudl is a widely adopted video analysis and performance review platform
used by teams across football, basketball and other sports. Its core
strength lies in video tagging, clip libraries and coach-to-athlete
feedback workflows.

\textbf{Key features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Video capture, tagging and playlists for match review.
  \item Performance reports built from tagged video events.
  \item Coach-to-athlete sharing of clips and feedback notes.
  \item Mobile companion app for athletes and coaches.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Focused almost entirely on video analysis --- no integrated
        medical, scouting, sponsorship or fan-facing modules.
  \item Limited support for multi-sport organisations operating
        football, basketball, handball and volleyball under one roof.
  \item No native role separation for sport manager, team manager,
        doctor, physio, fitness coach, scout, sponsor or fan.
  \item No embedded ML for match-outcome prediction or quantitative
        player rating.
  \item Pricing and deployment model targeted at clubs willing to
        commit to Hudl's hosted SaaS only.
\end{itemize}

\section{Catapult Sports / Sports One}
\label{sec:prevwork-catapult}
Catapult is best known for its wearable hardware that captures GPS,
heart-rate and accelerometer data during training. Sports One, its
analysis suite, surfaces athletic-load metrics back to coaching and
performance staff.

\textbf{Key features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Wearable-driven athlete monitoring (load, intensity,
        accelerations).
  \item Training-load dashboards and acute/chronic workload ratios.
  \item Recovery and readiness tracking from physiological signals.
  \item Integration with selected video analysis platforms.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Hardware-centric: requires per-athlete wearable devices to
        produce any value, with proprietary subscriptions.
  \item Does not manage contracts, transfers, sponsor offers, scouting
        prospects, fan content or in-game events --- it is a
        performance-data tool, not a club operating system.
  \item Limited role-based access tailored to the medical /
        performance team; sport managers, sponsors and fans are not
        first-class actors.
  \item No embedded sponsor approval workflow or fan portal.
\end{itemize}

\section{SAP Sports One}
\label{sec:prevwork-sap}
SAP Sports One is an enterprise sports management platform aimed at
top-tier clubs. It covers team administration, training, scouting and
medical workflows.

\textbf{Key features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Team administration, scouting and player development modules.
  \item Training and match planning with calendar integration.
  \item Medical and player-fitness tracking.
  \item Enterprise-grade integration with the SAP ecosystem.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Heavyweight enterprise pricing, integration overhead and SAP
        lock-in make it impractical for small or non-football clubs.
  \item Monolithic deployment model --- modules cannot be released
        independently the way an event-driven microservice platform
        can.
  \item No fan-facing surface; the platform is internal-only.
  \item Limited out-of-the-box ML for match prediction or player
        rating; analytics are built on top of SAP's BI stack at
        additional cost.
  \item Multi-sport coverage exists but is heavily football-biased and
        not as natively multi-discipline as the MSCMS data model.
\end{itemize}

\section{TeamSnap}
\label{sec:prevwork-teamsnap}
TeamSnap is a popular tool for amateur and semi-pro team coordination,
focused on schedules, attendance and communication.

\textbf{Key features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Game and practice scheduling with RSVPs.
  \item Roster management and contact directory.
  \item Team chat and message broadcasts.
  \item Mobile-first experience for parents and players.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Designed for grassroots / amateur teams --- not suitable for
        a professional multi-sport club with medical, scouting,
        sponsorship and analytics arms.
  \item No medical records, scout reports, sponsor contracts, or AI
        analytics.
  \item No role separation beyond \emph{coach}, \emph{player} and
        \emph{parent}.
  \item No event-streaming or pluggable ML inference back-end.
\end{itemize}

\section{Kitman Labs}
\label{sec:prevwork-kitman}
Kitman Labs is an athlete management platform aimed at integrating
medical, performance and analytics workflows for professional teams.

\textbf{Key features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Centralised athlete record combining medical and performance
        data.
  \item Injury-risk modelling and rehabilitation tracking.
  \item Dashboards aimed at medical and performance directors.
  \item Integrations with selected wearables and EHR providers.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Strong on medical / performance but does not cover sponsor
        offers, transfer workflows, fan portals, or in-game match
        operations end-to-end.
  \item Closed SaaS deployment; no equivalent of the open,
        Docker-Composed MSCMS topology that a club can self-host or
        adapt.
  \item Limited multi-sport modelling --- discipline structure is not
        as explicit as in the MSCMS \texttt{Sport / Team / Player /
        Position} data model.
  \item No embedded match-outcome prediction or generalisable player
        rating model surfaced directly to non-technical staff.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary of Gaps}
\label{sec:prevwork-gaps}
Taken together, existing platforms tend to specialise in one or two of
the eight MSCMS domains: video (Hudl), wearable performance
(Catapult), enterprise admin (SAP), grassroots coordination
(TeamSnap), or medical/performance integration (Kitman). None of them
combines:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Native multi-sport modelling across football, basketball,
        handball and volleyball.
  \item 19-role RBAC enforced consistently at identity provider,
        gateway and front-end middleware.
  \item Microservice decomposition with independent deployability and
        event-driven asynchronous fan-out via Kafka / RabbitMQ.
  \item Embedded ML services for match prediction and player rating
        served through the same origin as the UI.
  \item A fan-facing read-only surface integrated with the same
        platform that staff use internally.
  \item Open, Docker-Composed deployment that a club can adopt,
        extend or self-host.
\end{itemize}
These gaps motivate the design of MSCMS, presented in the chapters
that follow.
\fi

