\chapter{System Requirements Specification}
\label{ch:requirements}

\section{Functional Requirements}
\label{sec:functional-reqs}
MSCMS supports nineteen roles grouped into four permission tiers and
nine functional clusters. Each role sees a tailored navigation,
dashboard and CRUD surface enforced uniformly at the gateway and the
front-end middleware.

\subsection*{Onboarding and Authentication}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Onboarding Page:} Displays a landing page with the
        club identity, a brief description of MSCMS, and a
        \emph{Login} button.
  \item \textbf{Login:} Validates credentials against Keycloak using
        the OAuth 2.0 password grant. On success, the access token is
        decoded client-side; \texttt{sub} is stored as
        \texttt{keycloakId} and the user's role is stored in
        \texttt{localStorage} and as a cookie consumed by the Next.js
        middleware.
  \item \textbf{Refresh Token:} Transparent re-authentication via the
        refresh-token grant; on failure, the front-end clears storage
        and redirects to \texttt{/login}.
  \item \textbf{Role-Based Redirect:} After login, users land on a
        single \texttt{/dashboard} page whose contents are computed
        from their role.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Admin Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{User Management:} Create, edit, deactivate and delete
        users across all 19 roles via the user-management-service
        backed by Keycloak Admin API.
  \item \textbf{Staff Management:} Onboard sport managers, team
        managers, coaching staff, doctors, physios, scouts and
        sponsors with role assignment.
  \item \textbf{System-wide Dashboard:} Aggregated counts of users,
        teams, players, matches and outstanding tasks.
  \item \textbf{Notification Broadcasts:} Issue role-targeted alerts
        and announcements to any subset of users.
  \item \textbf{Audit View:} Read access to every domain (players,
        teams, training, medical, reports) for governance.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Sport Manager Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Multi-team Oversight:} View and manage every team
        across football, basketball, handball and volleyball.
  \item \textbf{Transfers:} Approve incoming transfers, outgoing
        transfers and call-ups; review scout reports.
  \item \textbf{Sponsor Contracts:} Review pending sponsor offers and
        accept or reject them.
  \item \textbf{Strategic Analytics:} Aggregated team and player
        performance across disciplines.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Team Manager Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Single-team Operations:} Manage roster, contracts,
        fixtures, line-ups and reports for one team.
  \item \textbf{Match Operations:} Create matches, attach formations,
        publish line-ups, log in-game events and request post-match
        reviews.
  \item \textbf{Training Plans:} Approve or adjust training plans
        proposed by the head coach.
  \item \textbf{Player Calls:} Issue call-ups to national teams.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Head Coach \& Assistant Coaches}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Training Sessions:} Create training plans, drills and
        sessions; assign player attendance and assessments.
  \item \textbf{Tactical Formations:} Design and save formations;
        publish line-ups for upcoming matches.
  \item \textbf{Match Events:} Record live in-game events (goals,
        cards, substitutions) and post-match reviews.
  \item \textbf{Player Stats:} View and analyse aggregated player
        performance per match and per training session.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Medical Staff (Doctor, Physio, Fitness Coach)}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Injury Records:} Log injuries, diagnoses, treatments
        and rehabilitations per player.
  \item \textbf{Recovery Programs:} Plan and track recovery programs
        with progress milestones.
  \item \textbf{Fitness Tests \& Training Load:} Capture fitness-test
        results and per-session training-load measurements.
  \item \textbf{Cross-domain Visibility:} Read access to training
        sessions and match minutes for context-aware rehab planning.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Scout Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Outer Players Catalogue:} Browse external prospects
        across rival teams and external markets.
  \item \textbf{Scout Reports:} Create, edit and delete scout reports
        with structured ratings and free-text observations.
  \item \textbf{Recommendation Pipeline:} Forward reports to sport
        manager for transfer consideration.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Sponsor Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Active Contracts:} View own contract status and
        history.
  \item \textbf{Submit Offers:} Submit new sponsorship offers with
        value, duration and conditions.
  \item \textbf{Notifications:} Receive decisions (accepted /
        rejected / pending) from sport manager.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Performance Analyst Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Match Analyses:} Author detailed analyses linked to
        matches with metrics, narrative and recommendations.
  \item \textbf{Player Analytics:} Compute and publish per-player
        analytics with KPIs spanning multiple matches.
  \item \textbf{Team Analytics:} Roll up team-level KPIs over a
        season.
  \item \textbf{AI Predictions:} Consume the ML services for match
        outcome prediction and player rating estimation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Player Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Personal Schedule:} View own training sessions and
        match fixtures.
  \item \textbf{Performance View:} Read-only access to own match
        statistics, training assessments and aggregated analytics.
  \item \textbf{Medical View:} Read-only view of own injury status,
        treatments and recovery progress.
  \item \textbf{Notifications:} Receive line-up announcements,
        call-ups, and training updates.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Fan Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Match Calendar:} View upcoming and past fixtures
        across all disciplines.
  \item \textbf{Teams \& Players Directory:} Read-only access to team
        rosters and player profiles.
  \item \textbf{Public Reports:} Read public-facing match analyses and
        team highlights.
  \item \textbf{Training Highlights:} Read-only view of public-facing
        training stories.
  \item \textbf{Restricted Surface:} Add / edit / delete buttons are
        hidden; medical, scouting, sponsor and finance views are
        invisible to fans.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{National Team Role}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Call-up Workflow:} Receive and accept call-ups for
        players from the club.
  \item \textbf{Outgoing Player View:} Read-only access to the
        scheduled training and match availability of called-up
        players.
\end{itemize}

\section{Non-Functional Requirements}
\label{sec:non-functional-reqs}

\subsection*{Technology Stack}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Front-end:} Next.js 16 (App Router) + React 19,
        Tailwind CSS v4, Barlow Condensed typography, react-icons,
        chart.js / Recharts, jwt-decode, js-cookie.
  \item \textbf{Back-end:} Java 17 + Spring Boot 3.x, Spring Cloud
        Config / Eureka / Gateway, Spring Data JPA + Hibernate.
  \item \textbf{Identity:} Keycloak 26.2.5 with the \texttt{mscms}
        realm and 19 realm roles.
  \item \textbf{Persistence:} PostgreSQL 14 (database-per-service:
        \texttt{mscms\_user}, \texttt{mscms\_player},
        \texttt{mscms\_training}, \texttt{mscms\_medical},
        \texttt{mscms\_notification}, \texttt{mscms\_reports}).
  \item \textbf{Messaging:} Apache Kafka 7.5.0 for high-volume event
        streams, RabbitMQ 3 for transactional notifications.
  \item \textbf{Cache:} Redis 7-alpine.
  \item \textbf{ML services:} Python 3 + FastAPI + Uvicorn, joblib
        model serialisation
        (\texttt{barca\_prediction\_model.joblib},
        \texttt{player\_evaluation\_model.joblib}).
  \item \textbf{Containerisation:} Docker Engine + Docker Compose
        (\texttt{docker-compose.full.yml}).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Security}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item JWT-based stateless authentication issued by Keycloak.
  \item Tokens verified at the gateway against Keycloak's JWKS
        endpoint, with realm-role claims propagated to downstream
        services via headers.
  \item Role-based access control enforced \emph{twice}: once at the
        gateway and once at the Next.js middleware (defence in depth).
  \item HTTPS terminations expected at the reverse proxy / load
        balancer in production deployments.
  \item Username / password regexes prevent invalid characters at
        creation time (matching Keycloak validation rules).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Performance}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Independent scaling per microservice --- the gateway and
        notification-mail-service can be horizontally scaled
        independently of the medical-fitness-service.
  \item Redis-backed caching for hot lookups (user profile by
        \texttt{keycloakId}).
  \item ML inference served behind a same-origin Next.js rewrite to
        avoid CORS pre-flights and to allow caching at the edge if
        desired.
  \item Idempotent seeders (\texttt{count() > 0} guard) so a redeploy
        does not duplicate data.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Scalability}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Horizontally scalable Spring Boot services discoverable
        through Eureka.
  \item Database-per-service prevents cross-domain lock contention
        and allows per-domain database scaling strategies (read
        replicas, partitioning).
  \item Event-driven fan-out via Kafka / RabbitMQ for high-volume
        notification streams.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Reliability}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Healthcheck endpoints on every service consumed by Docker
        Compose and ready to be wired into Kubernetes liveness /
        readiness probes.
  \item Friendly toast notifications surface API errors to the user
        without exposing stack traces.
  \item Idempotent seeders ensure consistent baseline state across
        redeployments.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Maintainability}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Single source of truth files for cross-cutting concerns:
        \texttt{permissions.js} on the front-end,
        \texttt{SeedIds.java} on the back-end.
  \item Each microservice has its own Maven module, Dockerfile and
        bootstrap seeder, allowing independent build and release.
  \item Spring Cloud Config externalises configuration so per-environment
        overrides do not require code changes.
  \item OpenAPI / Swagger UI shipped per service for live API
        exploration.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Observability}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Each Spring Boot service exposes
        \texttt{/actuator/health}, \texttt{/actuator/info} and
        \texttt{/actuator/metrics}.
  \item Eureka dashboard for service-registration visibility.
  \item Centralised application logs ready for Loki / ELK
        integration.
\end{itemize}

