\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{High-level MSCMS architecture. Solid arrows are synchronous HTTP;
         dashed arrows are control-plane (service registration, JWT
         validation).}
\label{fig:high-level}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Architectural Decisions}

\subsubsection{Why microservices?}
Each operational domain (medical, training, reports) has its own data
ownership, release cadence and access patterns. Decomposing along these
seams lets us:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item scale the high-traffic services (player, training-match) independently;
  \item evolve the schema of one domain without coordinating migrations
        across the whole codebase;
  \item rebuild and redeploy one service's container without touching the
        others.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Why Eureka + Config Server?}
The dynamic IPs assigned to Docker containers make hard-coded service URLs
brittle. Eureka acts as the source of truth for \emph{which container holds
which service right now}, and the Spring Cloud Config Server centralises
properties (database URLs, JWT issuer) so they can change without rebuilding
images.

\subsubsection{Why a separate Gateway?}
The gateway is the only externally-exposed Spring service. It validates JWT
tokens, applies global filters (CORS, logging), and routes by URI prefix
(\texttt{/players/**} $\to$ player-management-service). This keeps each
business service oblivious to authentication concerns and produces a single
public surface for the front-end to call.

\subsubsection{Why a Python ML tier?}
The match prediction and player rating models are pre-trained
\texttt{scikit-learn} pipelines serialised with \texttt{joblib}. Serving them
from Java would require a brittle bridge; instead, the same Docker image
hosts two FastAPI applications, one per model, communicating with the
front-end over a Next.js rewrite proxy to side-step CORS at the browser
boundary.

\subsection{Request Lifecycle}
A typical authenticated request follows this path:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Front-end attaches the JWT from \texttt{localStorage} as an
        \texttt{Authorization: Bearer} header and posts to the gateway.
  \item Gateway validates the JWT signature against Keycloak's JWKS, extracts
        the role claim, and routes by URI prefix.
  \item The downstream microservice executes the business logic, reads/writes
        its private PostgreSQL database, and returns the response.
  \item For asynchronous events (e.g.\ "injury reported"), the service
        publishes a Kafka topic that the notification service consumes to
        dispatch alerts.
\end{enumerate}

% ─── System Models / Microservices ──────────────────────────────────
\section{Microservices Decomposition}
\label{sec:services}

The system contains seven \textbf{Java/Spring Boot} services and two
\textbf{Python/FastAPI} services. The infrastructure containers
(PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka, RabbitMQ, Keycloak) are managed by the same
Compose stack but considered platform components rather than first-class
business microservices.

\subsection{Java microservices}

\begin{longtable}{p{0.27\textwidth} p{0.20\textwidth} p{0.45\textwidth}}
\toprule
\textbf{Service} & \textbf{DB} & \textbf{Responsibility} \\
\midrule\endhead

\texttt{eureka-server} & --- &
  Service registry. Every business service registers here on boot. \\

\texttt{config-server} & --- &
  Centralised \texttt{application.yml} distribution.
  Reads from a Git repo or classpath. \\

\texttt{gateway-service} & --- &
  Spring Cloud Gateway. Validates JWT, routes by URI prefix,
  applies CORS and logging filters. Public entry point on port \textbf{8080}. \\

\texttt{user-management-service} & \texttt{mscms\_user} &
  Owns the user-profile data for every role (\textbf{19 roles}).
  Talks to Keycloak Admin API to provision new users.
  Provides \texttt{/auth/login}, \texttt{/auth/signup} and
  \texttt{/auth/admin/create-user}. Also owns
  \texttt{NationalTeam} records. \\

\texttt{player-management-service} & \texttt{mscms\_player} &
  Sports, teams, rosters, contracts, outer (scouting) players,
  outer teams, incoming/outgoing transfers, and national-team call-ups. \\

\texttt{training-match-service} & \texttt{mscms\_training} &
  Training sessions, plans, drills, attendance, player training
  assessments. Match fixtures, formations, line-ups, in-game events,
  performance reviews, and per-player match statistics. \\

\texttt{medical-fitness-service} & \texttt{mscms\_medical} &
  Injuries, diagnoses, treatments, rehabilitation programs, recovery
  programs, fitness tests, and training loads. \\

\texttt{notification-mail-service} & \texttt{mscms\_notification} &
  In-app notifications, role-targeted alerts, and outbound e-mail.
  Subscribes to Kafka topics from the other services. \\

\texttt{reports-analytics-service} & \texttt{mscms\_reports} &
  Cross-domain analytics: match analyses, player analytics, team
  analytics, training analytics, scout reports, and sponsor contract
  offers. \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\subsection{Machine-learning services}

\begin{longtable}{p{0.30\textwidth} p{0.62\textwidth}}
\toprule
\textbf{Service} & \textbf{Behaviour} \\
\midrule
\texttt{ml-match-predictor} (port 9000) &
  FastAPI exposing \texttt{POST /predict} with
  \texttt{\{home\_team, away\_team, referee\_name\}}. Loads
  \texttt{FCB\_Football\_Matches.xlsx} and a pre-trained joblib model.
  Returns \texttt{\{prediction: W$|$D$|$L, probabilities\}}. \\
\texttt{ml-player-rating} (port 9001) &
  FastAPI exposing \texttt{GET /predict/\{player\_name\}}. Loads
  \texttt{Football\_Players\_Data.csv} (FC Barcelona squad) and a
  joblib regression model. Returns predicted rating and the actual
  dataset rating if available. \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\subsection{Inter-service communication}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Synchronous} REST through the gateway. The gateway forwards
        external traffic; internal-to-internal calls go via Eureka-resolved
        service IDs (e.g.\ \texttt{http://user-management-service/users}).
  \item \textbf{Asynchronous} Kafka topics. Producer services
        (training-match, medical-fitness) publish domain events
        (e.g.\ \texttt{InjuryReported}, \texttt{MatchFinished}); the
        notification service consumes them to fan out alerts and e-mails.
\end{itemize}

