\subsection{Workflow: AI Match Prediction}
Figure~\ref{fig:wf-predict} captures the simpler ML flow. Note that the
front-end calls the FastAPI service through a Next.js \emph{rewrite proxy}
to avoid CORS, not through the gateway.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    every node/.style={font=\scriptsize\sffamily},
    act/.style={draw, rounded corners=4pt, minimum width=3.6cm,
                minimum height=0.7cm, fill=barcaBlue!10, align=center},
    arr/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
    node distance=0.4cm
]
\node[act] (a) {Coach opens Match Predictor};
\node[act, below=of a] (b) {Selects home team, away team, referee};
\node[act, below=of b] (c) {Front-end posts to /ml-proxy/match/predict};
\node[act, below=of c] (d) {Next.js rewrite $\to$ http://ml-match-predictor:8000/predict};
\node[act, below=of d] (e) {FastAPI loads pre-trained joblib model};
\node[act, below=of e] (f) {Returns \{prediction: W$|$D$|$L, probabilities\}};
\node[act, below=of f] (g) {Front-end renders bars + outcome chip};
\draw[arr] (a)--(b); \draw[arr] (b)--(c); \draw[arr] (c)--(d);
\draw[arr] (d)--(e); \draw[arr] (e)--(f); \draw[arr] (f)--(g);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{AI match prediction workflow. The proxy hop is what makes the
         browser see the call as same-origin.}
\label{fig:wf-predict}
\end{figure}

