         medical and reports databases.}
\label{fig:er}
\end{figure}

% ─── System Models / Authentication Flow ────────────────────────────
\section{Authentication Flow}
\label{sec:auth}

\subsection{Identity provider}
Keycloak hosts the realm \texttt{mscms}, bootstrapped from
\texttt{keycloak/mscms-realm.json} at first boot. The realm declares 19
roles, an OIDC client \texttt{mscms-frontend} (public, standard flow +
direct grants enabled), and 16 seeded users covering every role.

\subsection{Login flow}
The login sequence is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:auth-seq}; the textual
walk-through is:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item The user submits \texttt{username + password} to the
        front-end's \texttt{Login} page.
  \item The front-end posts to \texttt{POST http://localhost:8080/auth/login}
        on the gateway.
  \item The gateway forwards to \texttt{user-management-service}, which calls
        Keycloak's \texttt{token} endpoint with the password grant.
  \item Keycloak returns a JWT \texttt{access\_token} (and a refresh token).
  \item The response propagates back to the browser; the front-end stores
        the access token in \texttt{localStorage} and the role
        (\texttt{realm\_access.roles[0]}) in both \texttt{localStorage} and
        an \texttt{HttpOnly}-style cookie consumed by Next.js middleware.
  \item Subsequent requests attach the JWT as
        \texttt{Authorization: Bearer <token>}; the gateway validates the
        signature against Keycloak's JWKS before forwarding.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Refresh-token flow}
Keycloak issues both an \texttt{access\_token} (short-lived,
default 7 days in \texttt{realm.json}) and a \texttt{refresh\_token}
(long-lived). When the access token expires, the front-end transparently
calls Keycloak's \texttt{token} endpoint again with
\texttt{grant\_type=refresh\_token} to obtain a fresh access token without
prompting the user. The refresh token itself is rotated on each call to
mitigate replay attacks.

\subsection{Role-based access control}
RBAC is enforced at two layers:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Edge enforcement} in Next.js middleware
        (\texttt{src/lib/permissions.js}): every dashboard route is mapped
        to an array of allowed roles; the request is short-circuited with
        a redirect when the role is missing.
  \item \textbf{Server-side enforcement} in Spring controllers: each endpoint
        is annotated with \texttt{@PreAuthorize} expressions that read the
        JWT's realm-role claim.
\end{enumerate}
The two layers use \emph{the same role list}, fed by a single source of
truth (the realm JSON for Keycloak, the permissions map for the front-end),
which makes role drift impossible by construction.

\subsection{Read-only mode for unprivileged roles}
The \texttt{fan} role is a special "consumer" role: it can view Players,
Teams, Matches, Training, Reports and the Dashboard, but every form's
\emph{Add}, \emph{Edit} and \emph{Delete} action is suppressed in the UI by
the \texttt{useRole()} hook, which returns \texttt{readOnly: true} for
roles listed in \texttt{READ\_ONLY\_ROLES}. This is purely a UX hint --- the
gateway still rejects any write attempt at the API level.

% ── Auth flow figure (TikZ + Mermaid source kept separately) ─────
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    every node/.style={font=\footnotesize\sffamily},
    actor/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2cm,
                  minimum height=0.7cm, fill=barcaBlue!12},
    flow/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[actor] (browser) {Browser};
\node[actor, right=2cm of browser] (gateway) {Gateway};
\node[actor, right=2cm of gateway] (umsvc) {User-Mgmt};
\node[actor, right=2cm of umsvc] (kc) {Keycloak};

\draw[flow] (browser.east) -- node[above]{\tiny POST /auth/login} (gateway.west);
\draw[flow] (gateway.east) -- node[above]{\tiny /auth/login} (umsvc.west);
\draw[flow] (umsvc.east) -- node[above]{\tiny /token (pwd grant)} (kc.west);
\draw[flow, dashed] (kc.west) to[bend left=20] node[below]{\tiny JWT} (umsvc.east);
\draw[flow, dashed] (umsvc.west) to[bend left=10] node[below]{\tiny JWT} (gateway.east);
\draw[flow, dashed] (gateway.west) to[bend left=10] node[below]{\tiny JWT} (browser.east);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Login sequence. Solid arrows are requests; dashed arrows are
         responses carrying the JWT.}
\label{fig:auth-seq}
\end{figure}

A Mermaid version of this sequence diagram, together with the refresh-token
flow, is provided in \texttt{AUTH\_SEQUENCE.md} for re-rendering.

% ─── System Models / Deployment View ────────────────────────────────
\section{Deployment View}
\label{sec:deploy}

This section documents how the logical microservices are mapped to
physical Docker containers, ports and networks. The whole stack is
launched by one command on a developer laptop or production host:
\begin{verbatim}
docker compose -f docker-compose.full.yml up -d
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Container Topology}
Figure~\ref{fig:deploy} maps every service in the Compose file to its
container, its image source, and its exposed host port (where applicable).
All containers join a single bridge network named \texttt{mscms-network},
which means service discovery via container name works out of the box.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
