% ── Frames go on a BACKGROUND layer so they don't cover the nodes ─
\begin{scope}[on background layer]
  \node[net, fit=(net),
        label={[font=\bfseries\small]above:Docker network \texttt{mscms-network}}] (netframe) {};
  \node[host, fit=(netframe),
        label={[font=\bfseries\small]below:Host machine
              (Docker Desktop, Windows/macOS/Linux)}] {};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Deployment topology. Boxes in blue are Java/Spring Boot services,
         red are Python/FastAPI ML services, grey are infrastructure
         containers. All boxes share one Docker bridge network.}
\label{fig:deploy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Build vs. Pull Strategy}
The Compose file specifies both an \texttt{image:} and a \texttt{build:}
directive for every business microservice. The dual directive lets
contributors choose:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \texttt{docker compose up} (no build) -- pulls the pre-built image
        from GHCR (\texttt{ghcr.io/tefaa1/mscms/*}). Fast for first-time
        evaluators.
  \item \texttt{docker compose build} -- builds from the local
        \texttt{Dockerfile} per service. Required after editing
        Java source. Each service's Dockerfile is a multi-stage build that
        produces a slim JRE image.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Volumes and Persistence}
Three named volumes survive container restarts:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \texttt{postgres\_mscms} -- holds all six MSCMS databases. Wiping
        it (\texttt{down -v}) is the only safe way to reset seed data.
  \item \texttt{keycloak\_data\_mscms} -- holds the imported realm,
        Keycloak's internal H2 (or external) DB, and any users created
        through the admin console.
  \item \texttt{redis\_data\_mscms}, \texttt{zookeeper\_data\_mscms}
        and \texttt{zookeeper\_log\_mscms} -- supporting infrastructure.
\end{itemize}

% =====================================================================
\chapter{Sequence Diagrams}
\label{ch:sequence}

This chapter collects every \emph{behavioural} model of MSCMS in one
place: per-role operation sequences (Admin, Sport Manager, Team
Manager, Head Coach, Doctor, Scout, Sponsor, Performance Analyst,
Player, Fan), authentication and refresh-token flows, machine-learning
prediction call paths, and the cross-cutting notification fan-out.
Static structural views live in the previous chapter
(Chapter~\ref{ch:system-models}). The diagrams use TikZ lifelines to
render the end-to-end flow; each diagram lists the participating actor
on the left and the participating services as vertical lifelines
across the page.

\section{Diagram Notation}
\label{sec:seq-notation}

Every sequence diagram in this chapter follows the same visual
conventions:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Lifelines.} The boxes along the top edge are the
        participants — actor on the left, then the services involved
        from left to right in roughly the order they are contacted.
        The dotted vertical lines underneath each box are the
        lifelines that anchor every message in time.
  \item \textbf{Solid arrows} represent \emph{requests} — synchronous
        method calls or HTTP requests where the sender expects a
        response.
  \item \textbf{Dashed arrows} represent \emph{responses} — the
        return value of a request, propagated back along the call
        stack.
  \item \textbf{Vertical position} is time: messages higher up
        happen before messages lower down. There is no implied unit
        of duration, only relative ordering.
  \item \textbf{Labels} on each arrow give the HTTP verb plus path
        (\texttt{POST /injuries}, \texttt{GET /players/\{id\}}, etc.)
        or the method name for in-process calls.
  \item \textbf{Asynchronous fan-out} (Kafka events, notification
        broadcasts) is shown as a separate arrow originating after
        the synchronous request has already completed, so the reader
        can see that the caller did not block on the fan-out.
  \item \textbf{Auth bookkeeping} (gateway JWT verification, role
        propagation as headers) is shown as a short label on the
        forwarding arrow rather than a separate trip; this keeps each
        diagram readable while still making the security boundary
        visible.
\end{itemize}

Where a particular call path is complex enough to obscure the higher
level intent, a short ``How it works'' paragraph follows the figure
walking through the steps in plain prose.

\section{Per-Role Sequence Diagrams}
\label{sec:impl-sequences}

\subsection*{Admin: Create a Staff User}
Figure~\ref{fig:seq-admin-create-staff} shows the path of a
\emph{Create staff user} request from the admin browser to Keycloak
and back into the application's profile tables.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={font=\footnotesize\sffamily},
  lane/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2.0cm,
               minimum height=0.65cm, fill=barcaBlue!12},
  msg/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
  ret/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, dashed, gray}
]
\node[lane] (A)  at (0,0)     {Admin (UI)};
\node[lane] (GW) at (3.0,0)   {Gateway};
\node[lane] (US) at (6.0,0)   {user-mgmt-svc};
\node[lane] (KC) at (9.0,0)   {Keycloak};
\node[lane] (DB) at (12.0,0)  {mscms\_user DB};

\foreach \n in {A,GW,US,KC,DB}
  \draw[dotted] (\n.south) -- ++(0,-6.6);

\draw[msg] ($(A.south)+(0,-0.5)$)  -- node[above]{\scriptsize POST /users \{role=DOCTOR\}} ($(GW.south)+(0,-0.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(GW.south)+(0,-1.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize forward + JWT verify} ($(US.south)+(0,-1.1)$);
\draw[msg] ($(US.south)+(0,-1.7)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize Admin API: create user} ($(KC.south)+(0,-1.7)$);
\draw[ret] ($(KC.south)+(0,-2.3)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 201 \{sub=uuid\}} ($(US.south)+(0,-2.3)$);
\draw[msg] ($(US.south)+(0,-2.9)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize assign role DOCTOR} ($(KC.south)+(0,-2.9)$);
\draw[ret] ($(KC.south)+(0,-3.5)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 204} ($(US.south)+(0,-3.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(US.south)+(0,-4.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize INSERT staff\_profile} ($(DB.south)+(0,-4.1)$);
\draw[ret] ($(DB.south)+(0,-4.7)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize OK} ($(US.south)+(0,-4.7)$);
\draw[ret] ($(US.south)+(0,-5.3)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 201 \{keycloakId\}} ($(GW.south)+(0,-5.3)$);
\draw[ret] ($(GW.south)+(0,-5.9)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 201} ($(A.south)+(0,-5.9)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Admin creates a staff user. Keycloak is the source of truth
         for identity; the profile row in \texttt{mscms\_user} is
         written only after Keycloak commits.}
\label{fig:seq-admin-create-staff}
\end{figure}

\textbf{How it works.} The admin fills the \emph{New User} modal and
submits. The browser sends a \texttt{POST /users} carrying the JSON
body and an \texttt{Authorization: Bearer} header. The gateway
verifies the signature against Keycloak's JWKS, checks that the
caller's realm role is \texttt{ADMIN}, and forwards the request to
\texttt{user-management-service}. The service calls Keycloak's Admin
API to provision the account (which generates the
\texttt{sub} UUID) and then assigns the requested role
(\texttt{DOCTOR} in this example). Only after Keycloak returns the
final \texttt{204} does the service open a transaction on
\texttt{mscms\_user} and insert the corresponding
\texttt{staff\_profile} row keyed by that UUID. If any of the three
calls fail, nothing is persisted on either side and the front-end
shows a toast with the precise error code returned by the gateway.

\subsection*{Admin: Broadcast a Notification}
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={font=\footnotesize\sffamily},
  lane/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2.0cm,
               minimum height=0.65cm, fill=barcaBlue!12},
  msg/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
  ret/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, dashed, gray}
]
\node[lane] (A)  at (0,0)    {Admin (UI)};
\node[lane] (GW) at (3.2,0)  {Gateway};
\node[lane] (NS) at (6.4,0)  {notif-svc};
\node[lane] (KQ) at (9.6,0)  {Kafka};
\node[lane] (U)  at (12.6,0) {Recipients};

\foreach \n in {A,GW,NS,KQ,U}
  \draw[dotted] (\n.south) -- ++(0,-4.6);

\draw[msg] ($(A.south)+(0,-0.5)$)  -- node[above]{\scriptsize POST /notifications/broadcast} ($(GW.south)+(0,-0.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(GW.south)+(0,-1.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize forward + JWT verify} ($(NS.south)+(0,-1.1)$);
\draw[msg] ($(NS.south)+(0,-1.7)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize publish event} ($(KQ.south)+(0,-1.7)$);
\draw[ret] ($(NS.south)+(0,-2.3)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 202 Accepted} ($(GW.south)+(0,-2.3)$);
\draw[ret] ($(GW.south)+(0,-2.9)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 202} ($(A.south)+(0,-2.9)$);
\draw[msg] ($(KQ.south)+(0,-3.5)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize fan-out (per role)} ($(U.south)+(0,-3.5)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Admin broadcasts a notification. The HTTP response returns
         immediately; Kafka takes care of asynchronous fan-out.}
\label{fig:seq-admin-broadcast}
\end{figure}

\textbf{How it works.} The broadcast modal collects the message body,
the priority level (low / normal / high / critical) and the target
audience (one role group, one user, or system-wide). The front-end
posts to \texttt{POST /notifications/broadcast}. The gateway
validates the JWT and forwards. The notification service does
\emph{not} fan out the notification itself on the request thread —
instead it publishes a single \texttt{NotificationBroadcast} event
onto Kafka and immediately returns \texttt{202 Accepted}. A Kafka
consumer running inside the same service then resolves the target
role list against \texttt{mscms\_user}, writes one row into
\texttt{notifications} per recipient, and (for priorities
\texttt{HIGH} or \texttt{CRITICAL}) issues SMTP sends through
\texttt{notification-mail-service}. Because the synchronous response
returns before fan-out completes, the admin UI feels instant even
when broadcasting to hundreds of users.

\subsection*{Sport Manager: Approve a Sponsor Offer}
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={font=\footnotesize\sffamily},
  lane/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2.0cm,
               minimum height=0.65cm, fill=barcaBlue!12},
  msg/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
  ret/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, dashed, gray}
]
\node[lane] (S)  at (0,0)    {Sport Mgr};
\node[lane] (GW) at (3.2,0)  {Gateway};
\node[lane] (RS) at (6.4,0)  {reports-svc};
\node[lane] (KQ) at (9.6,0)  {Kafka};
\node[lane] (NS) at (12.6,0) {notif-svc};

\foreach \n in {S,GW,RS,KQ,NS}
  \draw[dotted] (\n.south) -- ++(0,-4.8);

\draw[msg] ($(S.south)+(0,-0.5)$)  -- node[above]{\scriptsize PATCH /sponsors/\{id\} \{ACCEPTED\}} ($(GW.south)+(0,-0.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(GW.south)+(0,-1.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize forward + JWT verify} ($(RS.south)+(0,-1.1)$);
\draw[msg] ($(RS.south)+(0,-1.7)$) -- ++(1.0,0) -- ++(0,-0.3) -- ++(-1.0,0) node[midway, above, xshift=0.5cm]{\scriptsize UPDATE sponsor\_contract\_offers};
\draw[msg] ($(RS.south)+(0,-2.3)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize publish SponsorDecided} ($(KQ.south)+(0,-2.3)$);
\draw[ret] ($(RS.south)+(0,-2.9)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 200} ($(GW.south)+(0,-2.9)$);
\draw[ret] ($(GW.south)+(0,-3.5)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 200} ($(S.south)+(0,-3.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(KQ.south)+(0,-4.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize consume} ($(NS.south)+(0,-4.1)$);
\draw[msg] ($(NS.south)+(0,-4.7)$) -- ++(2.2,0) node[right]{\scriptsize notify sponsor};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Sport manager accepts a sponsor offer. The decision both
         updates the database row and emits a Kafka event consumed
         by the notification service.}
\label{fig:seq-sport-sponsor}
\end{figure}

\textbf{How it works.} The sport manager opens the Sponsorship page,
sees the pending offer in the \texttt{PENDING} column, and clicks
\emph{Accept}. The front-end sends a partial update via
\texttt{PATCH /sponsors/\{id\}} with body
\texttt{\{status: "ACCEPTED"\}}. The gateway propagates the JWT and
forwards to \texttt{reports-analytics-service}, which updates the
\texttt{sponsor\_contract\_offers} row inside a transaction and
publishes a \texttt{SponsorDecided} event on Kafka. The synchronous
response returns immediately to the sport manager so the UI flips the
chip from \texttt{PENDING} to \texttt{ACCEPTED} without waiting on
the notification fan-out. Downstream, the notification service
consumes the event and pushes an in-app notification (and an
optional email) to the originating sponsor account so they know
their offer was approved.

\subsection*{Team Manager: Create a Match}
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={font=\footnotesize\sffamily},
  lane/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2.0cm,
               minimum height=0.65cm, fill=barcaBlue!12},
  msg/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
  ret/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, dashed, gray}
]
\node[lane] (T)  at (0,0)    {Team Mgr};
\node[lane] (GW) at (3.2,0)  {Gateway};
\node[lane] (TM) at (6.4,0)  {training-match};
\node[lane] (DB) at (9.6,0)  {mscms\_training};
\node[lane] (KQ) at (12.6,0) {Kafka};

\foreach \n in {T,GW,TM,DB,KQ}
  \draw[dotted] (\n.south) -- ++(0,-4.6);

\draw[msg] ($(T.south)+(0,-0.5)$)  -- node[above]{\scriptsize POST /matches} ($(GW.south)+(0,-0.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(GW.south)+(0,-1.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize forward + JWT} ($(TM.south)+(0,-1.1)$);
\draw[msg] ($(TM.south)+(0,-1.7)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize INSERT matches} ($(DB.south)+(0,-1.7)$);
\draw[ret] ($(DB.south)+(0,-2.3)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize matchId=42} ($(TM.south)+(0,-2.3)$);
\draw[msg] ($(TM.south)+(0,-2.9)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize MatchCreated event} ($(KQ.south)+(0,-2.9)$);
\draw[ret] ($(TM.south)+(0,-3.5)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 201 \{matchId\}} ($(GW.south)+(0,-3.5)$);
\draw[ret] ($(GW.south)+(0,-4.1)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 201} ($(T.south)+(0,-4.1)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Team manager creates a match. A \texttt{MatchCreated} event
         on Kafka triggers downstream notifications and analytics
         pre-aggregation.}
\label{fig:seq-team-create-match}
\end{figure}

\textbf{How it works.} The team manager opens \emph{New Match} and
fills opponent, venue, kick-off time and competition. The form posts
to \texttt{POST /matches}. The gateway forwards to
\texttt{training-match-service}; the service starts a transaction,
inserts the row into the \texttt{matches} table, and emits the
generated \texttt{matchId} back through the chain. Before the
transaction commits, the service publishes a \texttt{MatchCreated}
event on Kafka carrying the new \texttt{matchId} and the originating
team. Two downstream consumers pick this event up: the notification
service announces the fixture to coaches and players, and the
reports-analytics service pre-creates a placeholder
\texttt{team\_analytics} row scoped to the new match so post-game
data writes are O(1) inserts rather than upsert lookups.

\subsection*{Head Coach: Create a Training Session}
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={font=\footnotesize\sffamily},
  lane/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2.0cm,
               minimum height=0.65cm, fill=barcaBlue!12},
  msg/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
  ret/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, dashed, gray}
]
\node[lane] (HC) at (0,0)   {Head Coach};
\node[lane] (GW) at (3.2,0) {Gateway};
\node[lane] (TM) at (6.4,0) {training-match};
\node[lane] (DB) at (9.6,0) {mscms\_training};
\node[lane] (NS) at (12.6,0){notif-svc};

\foreach \n in {HC,GW,TM,DB,NS}
  \draw[dotted] (\n.south) -- ++(0,-4.6);

\draw[msg] ($(HC.south)+(0,-0.5)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize POST /training/sessions} ($(GW.south)+(0,-0.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(GW.south)+(0,-1.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize forward} ($(TM.south)+(0,-1.1)$);
\draw[msg] ($(TM.south)+(0,-1.7)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize INSERT training\_sessions} ($(DB.south)+(0,-1.7)$);
\draw[ret] ($(DB.south)+(0,-2.3)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize sessionId} ($(TM.south)+(0,-2.3)$);
\draw[msg] ($(TM.south)+(0,-2.9)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize SessionScheduled} ($(NS.south)+(0,-2.9)$);
\draw[ret] ($(TM.south)+(0,-3.5)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 201} ($(GW.south)+(0,-3.5)$);
\draw[ret] ($(GW.south)+(0,-4.1)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize 201} ($(HC.south)+(0,-4.1)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Head coach schedules a training session. Players receive a
         \texttt{SessionScheduled} notification through the same
         fabric used for match line-ups.}
\label{fig:seq-coach-session}
\end{figure}

\textbf{How it works.} The coach opens \emph{New Session}, picks a
date, attaches drills from the drill library, and sets a target
duration. The form posts to \texttt{POST /training/sessions}. The
gateway forwards to \texttt{training-match-service}, which writes
the row into \texttt{training\_sessions} and emits a
\texttt{SessionScheduled} event directly to the notification service
(this is a synchronous in-process publish rather than a Kafka hop
because session creation runs at a moderate frequency that doesn't
justify a topic). The HTTP response carries the new
\texttt{sessionId} back to the coach so the UI can append the new
session to the day's strip without a refetch.

