\subsection*{Cross-cutting: Notification Fan-out on Domain Event}
Figure~\ref{fig:seq-notification-fanout} shows the most distinctive
cross-cutting pattern in MSCMS: any domain event (injury, sponsor
decision, transfer, match created) is dispatched by Kafka and
delivered both in-app and (optionally) by email.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={font=\footnotesize\sffamily},
  lane/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2.2cm,
               minimum height=0.65cm, fill=barcaBlue!12},
  ext/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum width=2.2cm,
              minimum height=0.65cm, fill=gray!15},
  msg/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick},
  ret/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]}, dashed, gray}
]
\node[lane] (P)  at (0,0)    {Producer svc};
\node[lane] (KQ) at (3.4,0)  {Kafka};
\node[lane] (NS) at (6.8,0)  {notif-svc};
\node[lane] (DB) at (10.0,0) {mscms\_notification};
\node[ext]  (EM) at (13.2,0) {SMTP};

\foreach \n in {P,KQ,NS,DB,EM}
  \draw[dotted] (\n.south) -- ++(0,-4.4);

\draw[msg] ($(P.south)+(0,-0.5)$)  -- node[above]{\scriptsize publish DomainEvent} ($(KQ.south)+(0,-0.5)$);
\draw[msg] ($(KQ.south)+(0,-1.1)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize consume} ($(NS.south)+(0,-1.1)$);
\draw[msg] ($(NS.south)+(0,-1.7)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize resolve target roles} ($(DB.south)+(0,-1.7)$);
\draw[ret] ($(DB.south)+(0,-2.3)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize keycloakIds[]} ($(NS.south)+(0,-2.3)$);
\draw[msg] ($(NS.south)+(0,-2.9)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize INSERT notifications (in-app)} ($(DB.south)+(0,-2.9)$);
\draw[msg] ($(NS.south)+(0,-3.5)$) -- node[above]{\scriptsize SEND (priority >= HIGH)} ($(EM.south)+(0,-3.5)$);
\draw[ret] ($(EM.south)+(0,-4.1)$) -- node[below]{\scriptsize ack} ($(NS.south)+(0,-4.1)$);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Notification fan-out. A single Kafka event becomes both an
         in-app notification row and an outbound email, with email
         restricted to priority \texttt{HIGH} or \texttt{CRITICAL}.}
\label{fig:seq-notification-fanout}
\end{figure}

% =====================================================================
\chapter{Implementation and Results}
\label{ch:implementation}

This chapter walks through every page of the MSCMS front-end, grouped
by the user role that owns it. Each subsection follows the
\textbf{Description / Key Features / Result} format and ties the
on-screen feature to the back-end service, database table and the
Keycloak role guarding it. Screenshots are placed inline so the reader
can match each feature to what the user actually sees on screen.

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Onboarding Page}

The onboarding surface is the public-facing landing page rendered at
\texttt{/} when the user is not yet authenticated. It introduces MSCMS
and routes the user into the login flow.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Hero section themed in the modelled club's brand colours
        (Barcelona blue \texttt{\#004D98} and red \texttt{\#A50044}).
  \item Brief platform pitch summarising the eight functional domains
        of the system.
  \item Single ``Login'' call-to-action that links to the
        \texttt{/login} route.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A first-time visitor immediately understands what
MSCMS is and where to authenticate. No data is fetched on this page,
keeping it fast and SEO-friendly even before login.

\screenshot{screenshots/onboarding.png}%
{Onboarding / landing page with the Barcelona-themed hero and the
 \emph{Login} call-to-action.}{fig:ss-onboarding}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Login Page}

\textbf{Description:} The login page allows any of the 19 roles to
authenticate against Keycloak using the OAuth 2.0 password grant. The
form validates input client-side, then posts
\texttt{\{username, password\}} to
\texttt{POST /auth/login} on the gateway, which forwards to
\texttt{user-management-service} and on to Keycloak.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Input validation (non-empty username, non-empty password).
  \item Friendly toast feedback for \texttt{401 Unauthorized}
        responses.
  \item Decodes the returned JWT (\texttt{jwt-decode}) to store
        \texttt{token}, \texttt{keycloakId} (\texttt{sub} claim) and
        \texttt{user\_role} (mapped from \texttt{realm\_access.roles})
        in \texttt{localStorage}, plus a same-name cookie for the
        Next.js middleware.
  \item Redirects to \texttt{/dashboard} on success.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Security:} JWT signature is verified server-side at every
subsequent request by the gateway against Keycloak's JWKS endpoint.
Username regex (\texttt{/\^{}[a-zA-Z0-9.\_-]+\$/}) mirrors Keycloak's
own validation rules to prevent \texttt{error-username-invalid-character}
responses.

\textbf{Result:} A reliable, role-aware single-sign-on surface that
issues stateless tokens consumed identically by both the front-end
middleware and the back-end gateway.

\screenshot{screenshots/login.png}%
{Login page. The same form is reused by every one of the 19 roles;
 the destination dashboard is computed from the JWT after success.}%
{fig:ss-login}

% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Forgot Password / Refresh-Token Flow}

\textbf{Description:} While password reset itself is handled by
Keycloak's own account console, the front-end implements transparent
session refresh: if any API call returns \texttt{401}, the front-end
posts \texttt{POST /auth/refresh \{refresh\_token\}} and silently
retries the original call. If the refresh token has been revoked, the
user is logged out and redirected to \texttt{/login}.

\textbf{Result:} Long-lived sessions remain stable without forcing
users to re-enter credentials every hour, while expired or revoked
tokens fail safely.

% =====================================================================
\section{Admin Role Implementation}
\label{sec:impl-admin}

The Admin role has the broadest reach in MSCMS. It governs system-wide
user lifecycle, has read access to every operational domain and can
issue broadcast notifications.

\subsection*{Admin Dashboard}

\textbf{Description:} The admin landing surface aggregates counts
across all six databases through parallel calls to
\texttt{user-management-service},
\texttt{player-management-service},
\texttt{training-match-service},
\texttt{medical-fitness-service},
\texttt{reports-analytics-service} and
\texttt{notification-mail-service}.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Top-line counts: total users, total players, total teams,
        total matches.
  \item Per-discipline pie chart (football, basketball, handball,
        volleyball) computed from the player roster, using the
        \texttt{POSITION\_TO\_SPORT} map mirrored from the back-end
        \texttt{Position} enum.
  \item Recent activity feed sourced from the notification service.
  \item Quick-action chips routing to user management, staff
        management and notification broadcasts.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} The admin has a real-time pulse of the platform on a
single screen, with one-click drill-down into every operational
surface.

\screenshot{screenshots/admin-dashboard.png}%
{Admin dashboard aggregating counts from six microservices into a
 single overview.}{fig:ss-admin-dashboard}

\subsection*{Users Management Page}

\textbf{Description:} CRUD over all 19 roles, surfaced through
\texttt{user-management-service} which proxies the Keycloak Admin
API and persists role-specific profile rows (e.g.\
\texttt{player\_profile}, \texttt{staff\_profile}) under a single
\texttt{keycloakId}.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Filter by role and search by username / email.
  \item Create user modal with role picker, username regex validation
        and immediate Keycloak provisioning.
  \item Edit profile data without losing the Keycloak identity.
  \item Deactivate / delete users (cascades into Keycloak realm
        revocation and downstream profile removal).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A single source of truth for user identity that
keeps Keycloak and the application databases consistent through every
mutation.

\screenshot{screenshots/admin-users.png}%
{Users Management page: role filter, search, and the create-user
 modal driven by the user-management-service.}{fig:ss-admin-users}

\subsection*{Staff Management Page}

\textbf{Description:} Specialised CRUD for non-player staff
(Sport Manager, Team Manager, Head Coach, Assistant Coach, Doctor,
Physio, Fitness Coach, Scout, Sponsor, Performance Analyst). Same
back-end as Users Management, but the modal pre-filters role choices
to staff roles and exposes staff-specific fields (specialty, licence
number, team assignment).

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Role chip column that colour-codes each staff member by
        functional area.
  \item Team assignment dropdown sourced live from
        \texttt{player-management-service / teams}.
  \item Bulk activate / deactivate for end-of-season offboarding.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} Streamlined onboarding of every non-player actor in
the club, with role assignment and team binding in a single workflow.

\screenshot{screenshots/admin-staff.png}%
{Staff Management page with the role-coloured chip column.}%
{fig:ss-admin-staff}

\subsection*{Notifications Page (Admin View)}

\textbf{Description:} Broadcast surface backed by
\texttt{notification-mail-service}. Admins can issue notifications to
any subset of roles or to a specific user.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Targeted dispatch by role group, by individual user, or
        system-wide.
  \item Priority levels (low / normal / high / critical) reflected in
        the UI styling for recipients.
  \item Delivery channels (in-app, email) chosen per broadcast.
  \item History list of past broadcasts with delivery counts.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A reliable broadcast plane for governance
announcements without requiring email-server access.

\screenshot{screenshots/notifications-admin.png}%
{Admin broadcast view with priority chips and per-role targeting.}%
{fig:ss-admin-notif}

\subsection*{System-wide Read Access}

The admin role passes the gateway's authorisation check for every
read endpoint --- players, teams, matches, training, medical reports,
analytics, sponsor contracts --- so admins can audit any data in the
system from the same UI used by domain specialists.

