\section{Sport Manager Role Implementation}
\label{sec:impl-sport-manager}

The Sport Manager owns club-wide strategic decisions across every
discipline.

\subsection*{Sport Manager Dashboard}

\textbf{Description:} Cross-discipline KPI surface that aggregates
data from \texttt{reports-analytics-service} and rolls up team-level
analytics across football, basketball, handball and volleyball.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Per-discipline win/draw/loss tally.
  \item Pending sponsor offers count with one-click navigation to
        the Sponsorship page.
  \item Transfers awaiting approval (incoming, outgoing, call-ups).
  \item Scout-report inbox preview.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} The sport manager sees every cross-cutting decision
queued up for their attention in one place.

\screenshot{screenshots/sport-mgr-dashboard.png}%
{Sport Manager dashboard with per-discipline KPI cards and the
 pending sponsor/transfer queue.}{fig:ss-sport-dashboard}

\subsection*{Transfers Page}

\textbf{Description:} Manages the three transfer types modelled in
\texttt{player-management-service}: \emph{incoming} (a player joining
from outside the club), \emph{outgoing} (a player leaving), and
\emph{call-ups} (a player loaned to a national team).

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Tabbed view: Incoming / Outgoing / Call-ups.
  \item Status badges: \texttt{PENDING}, \texttt{ACCEPTED},
        \texttt{REJECTED}, \texttt{COMPLETED}.
  \item Approve / reject actions that emit a Kafka event picked up
        by the notification service.
  \item Link to the underlying scout report (for incoming) or
        national-team call-up record.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A complete audit trail for every player movement,
linked back to the scouting and notification fabrics.

\screenshot{screenshots/transfers.png}%
{Transfers page showing the Incoming / Outgoing / Call-up tabs and
 status pipeline.}{fig:ss-transfers}

\subsection*{Sponsors Page}

\textbf{Description:} CRUD over the \texttt{sponsor\_contract\_offers}
table in \texttt{mscms\_reports}. Sponsor offers are created by users
with the \emph{Sponsor} role and queued for sport-manager approval.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Status pipeline view: \texttt{PENDING},
        \texttt{ACCEPTED}, \texttt{REJECTED}.
  \item Offer detail panel showing value, duration, conditions and
        the originating sponsor company.
  \item Accept / reject buttons that update the database and emit a
        notification to the originating sponsor.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} The Spotify and Nike offers in the demo seed land
\texttt{ACCEPTED}, while Damm and Beko sit \texttt{PENDING} ---
demonstrating the full workflow end-to-end with deterministic data.

\screenshot{screenshots/sponsors.png}%
{Sponsors page with the Spotify / Nike / Damm / Beko offers in the
 demo seed data.}{fig:ss-sponsors}

\subsection*{Cross-discipline Player and Team Views}

The Sport Manager has read access to every team and player across
disciplines, including detailed analytics roll-ups not available to
team managers.

% =====================================================================
\section{Team Manager Role Implementation}
\label{sec:impl-team-manager}

The Team Manager owns one specific team within one discipline and is
the operational counterpart to the Head Coach.

\subsection*{Team Manager Dashboard}

\textbf{Description:} Single-team operational view scoped to the
team the manager is assigned to.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Roster snapshot with active / injured counts.
  \item Upcoming fixtures with status badges.
  \item Pending training plans awaiting approval.
  \item Recent match events.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A focused, single-team cockpit without the
cross-discipline noise of the Sport Manager view.

\screenshot{screenshots/team-mgr-dashboard.png}%
{Team Manager dashboard scoped to a single team.}%
{fig:ss-team-dashboard}

\subsection*{Players Page}

\textbf{Description:} Single-team roster CRUD with positions filtered
to the discipline (e.g.\ handball positions are
\texttt{HB\_LEFT\_WING}, \texttt{HB\_RIGHT\_WING}, etc.).

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Add Player modal posts to \texttt{POST /players} with a
        17-field schema that provisions both a Keycloak user and a
        \texttt{player\_profile} row in one call.
  \item Position dropdown bound to the discipline (football positions
        for FC Barcelona, basketball positions for B\`asquet, handball
        positions for Handbol, volleyball positions for CV).
  \item Edit player with form pre-filled from
        \texttt{GET /players/\{id\}}.
  \item Contract status and rehabilitation banners surface medical
        flags inline.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A discipline-aware roster surface where adding a
player creates both an identity and a profile in one round-trip,
preventing the orphan-record drift typical of Keycloak-backed apps.

\screenshot{screenshots/players.png}%
{Players page with the discipline filter chips and the Add Player
 modal.}{fig:ss-players}

\subsection*{Teams Page}

\textbf{Description:} List of every team across disciplines. Team
Managers see read-only entries for teams other than their own, while
Sport Managers and Admins can edit any team.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Discipline filter chips with the
        \texttt{MdOutlineSportsVolleyball} icon for the Teams
        navigation entry.
  \item Crest, founding year and coach summary per team.
  \item Drill-down to roster, schedule and historical analytics.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A consistent multi-discipline directory that respects
team-manager scope.

\screenshot{screenshots/teams.png}%
{Teams page covering FC Barcelona, B\`asquet, Tennis Academy, CV and
 Handbol.}{fig:ss-teams}

\subsection*{Matches Page}

\textbf{Description:} Lists matches owned by the team. Team Managers
can create new fixtures, attach formations, publish line-ups and log
post-match reviews.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Status pipeline: \texttt{SCHEDULED}, \texttt{LIVE},
        \texttt{FINISHED}.
  \item Match detail panel showing opponent, venue, kick-off,
        formation and assigned line-up.
  \item Event log for goals, cards, substitutions (head coach
        edits, team manager reads).
  \item Linked post-match analysis from the Performance Analyst.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} Every match from the demo seed (FC Barcelona vs.\
Real Madrid \texttt{FINISHED}, vs.\ Atl\'etico \texttt{SCHEDULED},
basketball vs.\ RM Baloncesto) is visible end-to-end with formations
and events.

\screenshot{screenshots/matches.png}%
{Matches page with the status pipeline and the per-match detail
 panel.}{fig:ss-matches}

