\section{Performance Analyst Role Implementation}
\label{sec:impl-analyst}

The Performance Analyst is the heaviest consumer of the
\texttt{reports-analytics-service} and the ML services.

\subsection*{Analytics Page}

\textbf{Description:} Overview surface that aggregates five back-end
calls in parallel: team analytics, player analytics, match analytics,
training analytics and active injuries. The result is denormalised in
the front-end into a single canvas.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Headline KPIs: wins, goals scored, goals conceded, injuries.
  \item Player leaderboards (top scorers, top assists, fewest minutes
        on the bench).
  \item Per-team form curves over the season.
  \item Training-load distribution histogram.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} The Analytics Overview becomes the single source of
truth for board-level reporting, replacing what would otherwise be
multiple spreadsheets and reports.

\screenshot{screenshots/analytics.png}%
{Analytics Overview aggregating five APIs into one canvas.}%
{fig:ss-analytics}

\subsection*{Match Analyses Page}

\textbf{Description:} Structured per-match analysis authored by the
Performance Analyst, linked to the underlying match.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Pre-filled metrics from the match-event log.
  \item Free-text narrative section with embedded headings.
  \item Recommendations list consumed by the coaching staff.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} The 1 demo match analysis is visible to coaches,
team managers and sport managers --- a single document threaded
through the workflow.

\screenshot{screenshots/match-analysis.png}%
{Match Analyses page with the narrative-plus-metrics layout.}%
{fig:ss-match-analysis}

\subsection*{AI \& Predictions Page}

\textbf{Description:} Front-end for the two FastAPI ML services:
\texttt{ml-match-predictor} (port 9000) and
\texttt{ml-player-rating} (port 9001). The front-end calls them
through a Next.js rewrite proxy (\texttt{/ml-proxy/match/*} and
\texttt{/ml-proxy/player/*}) to avoid CORS pre-flights.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Match prediction form: home team, away team, referee
        selector with an \emph{Other} fallback.
  \item Player rating form bounded to a curated list of 20 known
        players from the training dataset (avoids
        \emph{Player not found} errors).
  \item Confidence / probability display.
  \item Loading and error states with toast feedback.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} Non-technical staff consume ML predictions through
the same UI as any other domain feature, with no awareness of the
Python services running underneath.

\screenshot{screenshots/ai-match-predict.png}%
{AI Match Predictor: home/away selectors, referee picker and the
 outcome probability bars.}{fig:ss-ai-match}

\screenshot{screenshots/ai-player-rating.png}%
{AI Player Rating: known-players autocomplete and the predicted vs.\
 actual rating.}{fig:ss-ai-player}

\subsection*{Reports Page}

\textbf{Description:} Browseable list of structured reports (match
analyses, team analytics, training analytics) with download support.
The \texttt{handleDownload} action generates a \texttt{.txt} blob
client-side from the JSON payload.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Filter by report type and date range.
  \item One-click download to \texttt{.txt}.
  \item Linked source records (match, team, training session).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A self-service report library that does not require
the analyst to copy data into Word / PDF for every consumer.

\screenshot{screenshots/reports.png}%
{Reports page with download-to-\texttt{.txt} actions.}{fig:ss-reports}

