\section{Player Role Implementation}
\label{sec:impl-player}

Players are first-class users with read-only access to their own
performance, schedule and medical state.

\subsection*{Player Dashboard}

\textbf{Description:} Personal cockpit: today's session, next match,
last assessment, current medical status.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Today's training session card with attendance state.
  \item Next match card with countdown.
  \item Latest assessment summary (coach-authored).
  \item Active injury / rehab status (medical-authored, read-only).
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A player sees the operational state of their own
career on one screen, without needing to ask staff.

\screenshot{screenshots/player-dashboard.png}%
{Player Dashboard: today's session, next match, latest assessment
 and active medical status.}{fig:ss-player-dashboard}

\subsection*{Personal Stats Page}

\textbf{Description:} Aggregated per-player stats sourced from the
\texttt{match\_events} and \texttt{player\_match\_statistics} tables.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Goals, assists, minutes per match.
  \item Training attendance percentage.
  \item Trend line over the season.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} Transparent, real-time access to their own
performance --- empowering players to negotiate, self-assess and stay
motivated.

\subsection*{Personal Medical View}

\textbf{Description:} Read-only view of own injury, diagnosis,
treatment and rehab records.

\textbf{Result:} Player can follow their own recovery journey without
asking medical staff for daily updates.

% =====================================================================
\section{Fan Role Implementation}
\label{sec:impl-fan}

Fans have a curated read-only surface: Matches, Players, Teams,
Training (public-facing parts only) and Reports (public parts only).
Medical, scouting, sponsorship and finance views are completely
hidden from the navigation, and all \emph{Add / Edit / Delete}
buttons are removed across pages where the Fan still has read access.

\subsection*{Fan Dashboard}

\textbf{Description:} Public-facing club hub.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Upcoming fixtures across all disciplines.
  \item Latest match result.
  \item Player of the week (sourced from analytics).
  \item Club news strip.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} Fans get an integrated club experience inside the
same platform staff use --- a feature competitors like Hudl and
Catapult do not provide.

\screenshot{screenshots/fan-dashboard.png}%
{Fan Dashboard: read-only club hub.}{fig:ss-fan-dashboard}

\subsection*{Fan Matches / Players / Teams / Training / Reports Pages}

Each of these pages reuses the same React component as the
corresponding staff page, but with conditional rendering driven by
the \texttt{canAccess} helper from \texttt{permissions.js}:
\emph{Add / Edit / Delete} buttons are hidden, edit-only fields are
collapsed and the breadcrumbs do not expose internal-only entities
(scout reports, sponsor offers, injuries).

\textbf{Result:} A polished public surface that shares its components
with the staff surface --- new fields added to staff views appear
automatically (read-only) to fans, with no double maintenance.

\screenshot{screenshots/fan-pages.png}%
{The same Matches component rendered for a fan: Add / Edit / Delete
 actions are suppressed by \texttt{canAccess}.}{fig:ss-fan-pages}

% =====================================================================
\section{Notifications (All Roles)}
\label{sec:impl-notifications}

\textbf{Description:} Bell icon in the navbar opens a panel powered
by \texttt{notification-mail-service}. Notifications are produced by
back-end Kafka consumers on key events (transfer decision, injury
opened, sponsor decision, line-up published) and fanned out to the
relevant role group.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item In-app feed with unread counter.
  \item Priority styling (low / normal / high / critical).
  \item Optional email delivery for high / critical notifications.
  \item Per-user read state persisted by the service.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} Every staff role and every player receives the right
information at the right moment without the noise of a club-wide
mailing list.

\screenshot{screenshots/notifications-panel.png}%
{Navbar notifications panel with unread counter and priority
 styling.}{fig:ss-notifications-panel}

% =====================================================================
\section{Settings (All Roles)}
\label{sec:impl-settings}

\textbf{Description:} The settings page presents a tabbed view of
profile, security and preferences. \texttt{ProfileTab} reads
\texttt{name}, \texttt{email} and \texttt{role} from the decoded JWT,
guaranteeing that what the user sees is exactly what Keycloak
believes about them.

\textbf{Key Features:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Profile view sourced from the JWT plus profile-row
        enrichment.
  \item Password change link redirecting to Keycloak account console.
  \item Language and theme preferences stored client-side.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Result:} A consistent identity surface across all 19 roles,
with no drift between the JWT and the visible profile.

\screenshot{screenshots/settings.png}%
{Settings page: Profile tab driven by the decoded JWT.}%
{fig:ss-settings}

% =====================================================================
\section{Cross-cutting Implementation Notes}
\label{sec:impl-crosscutting}

\textbf{Permissions source of truth:}
\texttt{sports-club-main/my-app/src/lib/permissions.js} contains
\texttt{SIDEBAR\_SECTIONS} (the navigation map) and
\texttt{canAccess} (the per-route role check). Both the
\texttt{Sidebar} component and the Next.js middleware consume this
file --- there is no second implementation of the rules.

\textbf{Idempotent seeders:}
Every microservice has a \texttt{*DataSeeder.java} bootstrap class
guarded by a \texttt{count() > 0} check. Restarting the stack does
not duplicate data. The notification service got a brand-new
\texttt{NotificationDataSeeder} created from scratch for this
project; the others were extended to seed FC Barcelona's real
squads, contracts, transfers, sponsor offers and analytics.

\textbf{Single docker-compose:}
\texttt{MSCMS-main/docker-compose.full.yml} brings the entire stack
up. \texttt{build:} directives ensure local source is rebuilt; ML
services are included on ports 9000 and 9001.

\textbf{Front-end ML proxy:}
\texttt{sports-club-main/my-app/next.config.mjs} rewrites
\texttt{/ml-proxy/match/*} to \texttt{http://localhost:9000} and
\texttt{/ml-proxy/player/*} to \texttt{http://localhost:9001},
avoiding CORS pre-flights when calling Python from the browser.

\textbf{Result of Implementation:}
The system is end-to-end functional with deterministic FC Barcelona
seed data, 19 working roles, full RBAC at gateway and middleware,
embedded ML predictions and a polished UX --- delivering on every
goal listed in Section~\ref{sec:goals}.

% =====================================================================
\chapter{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{ch:conclusion}

\section*{Multi-Sport Club Management System}

MSCMS is an integrated platform designed to overcome the limitations
of existing sports management tools, offering a unified solution for
multi-discipline club operations and analytics. Key features include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Nineteen User Roles}: Tailored dashboards across
        leadership, coaching, medical, scouting, commercial and
        end-user actors.
  \item \textbf{Multi-Sport Modelling}: Native support for football,
        basketball, handball and volleyball under a single data
        model.
  \item \textbf{Microservice Architecture}: Seven Spring Boot
        services + two FastAPI services with Eureka discovery and a
        single API gateway.
  \item \textbf{Embedded ML Predictions}: Match outcome and player
        rating served behind a same-origin proxy.
  \item \textbf{Centralised Analytics}: One Overview page aggregating
        five APIs.
  \item \textbf{Fan-facing Surface}: Read-only public hub integrated
        in the same platform staff use.
  \item \textbf{Event-driven Notifications}: Kafka- and
        RabbitMQ-backed role-aware fan-out.
\end{itemize}

\section{Comparison Between Sports Management Platforms}
\label{sec:comparison-table}

\begin{longtable}{p{0.30\textwidth} p{0.10\textwidth} p{0.13\textwidth} p{0.13\textwidth} p{0.10\textwidth} p{0.10\textwidth}}
  \toprule
  \textbf{Feature / Tool} & \textbf{Hudl} & \textbf{Catapult} &
  \textbf{SAP Sports One} & \textbf{TeamSnap} & \textbf{MSCMS} \\
  \midrule
  19-role RBAC (sport mgr, doctor, scout, sponsor, fan, \ldots) &
  Limited & Limited & Partial & Limited & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Multi-sport modelling (football + basketball + handball + volleyball) &
  Limited & Limited & Partial & Limited & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Microservice architecture (Eureka + gateway + Kafka) &
  No & No & Partial & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Embedded ML match prediction &
  No & No & Partial & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Embedded ML player rating &
  No & Partial & Partial & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Medical \& fitness module (injuries, rehab, fitness tests) &
  No & Partial & Yes & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Scout reports + outer players catalogue &
  No & No & Yes & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Sponsor offers workflow &
  No & No & Limited & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Transfer / call-up workflow &
  No & No & Partial & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Fan-facing surface in same platform &
  No & No & No & Limited & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Self-hostable (Docker Compose) &
  No & No & No & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \midrule
  Open architecture (Spring Boot + Next.js + FastAPI) &
  No & No & No & No & \textbf{Yes} \\
  \bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\section{Conclusion}

The analysis of Hudl, Catapult, SAP Sports One, TeamSnap and Kitman
Labs revealed critical gaps in role coverage, multi-sport modelling,
embedded ML and integrated fan-facing surfaces. MSCMS surpasses these
platforms by offering a single coherent system that combines nineteen
roles, four disciplines, seven microservices and two ML services
behind a single API gateway and a polished Next.js UI. By addressing
the fragmentation and analytical blindness identified in
Section~\ref{sec:problem-definition}, MSCMS delivers an innovative
solution that streamlines club operations and fosters cross-domain
decision-making.

\section{What was delivered}
This project delivered a working multi-sport club management platform that:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item Decomposes the back-end into seven Java microservices, two
        Python ML services and supporting infrastructure containers,
        all orchestrated by a single Docker Compose file.
  \item Authenticates every request against a Keycloak realm with 19
        roles, gating both the front-end navigation and the back-end
        endpoints with consistent role lists.
  \item Persists data across six PostgreSQL databases using the
        database-per-service pattern, with deterministic seed data that
        models FC Barcelona's real organisational structure.
  \item Exposes a polished Next.js front-end with role-aware navigation,
        read-only fan mode, aggregated analytics, downloadable reports,
        AI predictions, and the full CRUD surface of every operational
        domain.
\end{itemize}

\section{Lessons learned}
The most expensive lesson was the cost of \emph{contract drift} between
the front-end, the back-end DTOs and the database schema. We resolved it
by treating one file (\texttt{permissions.js} on the front-end and
\texttt{SeedIds.java} on the back-end) as the source of truth for each
cross-cutting concern. A second lesson was the importance of
\emph{idempotent seeders}: every service guards its seed code with a
\texttt{count() > 0} check, which makes the system safe to redeploy
without wiping volumes.

\section{Future work}
Several extensions would naturally fit on top of the existing
architecture:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Real-time match feed} via Server-Sent Events from
        training-match-service, surfaced as live tickers in the
        front-end.
  \item \textbf{Centralised observability} via Prometheus + Grafana
        dashboards reading from each service's \texttt{actuator/prometheus}
        endpoint.
  \item \textbf{Distributed tracing} with OpenTelemetry / Jaeger to make
        cross-service request paths visible.
  \item \textbf{Production ML pipeline} that retrains the prediction
        models on fresh match data, replacing the static joblib snapshots
        with a versioned model registry.
\end{itemize}

